{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08776","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08776","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.08776","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kimaya","familyName":"Basu","name":"Basu, Kimaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Savi","familyName":"Kolari","name":"Kolari, Savi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Allison","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Allison","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Measuring Moral LLM Responses in Multilingual Capacities"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-09T19:47:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:07:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With LLM usage becoming widespread across countries, languages, and humanity more broadly, the need to understand and guardrail their multilingual responses increases. Large-scale datasets for testing and benchmarking have been created to evaluate and facilitate LLM responses across multiple dimensions. In this study, we evaluate the responses of frontier and leading open-source models in five dimensions across low and high-resource languages to measure LLM accuracy and consistency across multilingual contexts. We evaluate the responses using a five-point grading rubric and a judge LLM. Our study shows that GPT-5 performed the best on average in each category, while other models displayed more inconsistency across language and category. Most notably, in the Consent \u0026amp; Autonomy and Harm Prevention \u0026amp; Safety categories, GPT scored the highest with averages of 3.56 and 4.73, while Gemini 2.5 Pro scored the lowest with averages of 1.39 and 1.98, respectively. These findings emphasize the need for further testing on how linguistic shifts impact LLM responses across various categories and improvement in these areas."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 5 figures; referenced articles: arXiv:2303.08774, arXiv:2303.12528, arXiv:2308.14132, arXiv:2505.12201, arXiv:2406.04428, arXiv:2407.02273, arXiv:2404.01268, arXiv:2502.09747, arXiv:2507.13474, arXiv:2505.21479, arXiv:2306.05685"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08776","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-13T01:44:29Z","registered":"2025-10-13T01:44:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T01:44:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08769","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08769","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.08769","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Proggya","familyName":"Chakraborty","name":"Chakraborty, Proggya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaquib","familyName":"Asrar","name":"Asrar, Aaquib","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jayasree","familyName":"Sengupta","name":"Sengupta, Jayasree","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sipra Das","familyName":"Bit","name":"Bit, Sipra Das","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prioritizing Latency with Profit: A DRL-Based Admission Control for 5G Network Slices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-09T19:36:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:07:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"5G networks enable diverse services such as eMBB, URLLC, and mMTC through network slicing, necessitating intelligent admission control and resource allocation to meet stringent QoS requirements while maximizing Network Service Provider (NSP) profits. However, existing Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) frameworks focus primarily on profit optimization without explicitly accounting for service delay, potentially leading to QoS violations for latency-sensitive slices. Moreover, commonly used epsilon-greedy exploration of DRL often results in unstable convergence and suboptimal policy learning. To address these gaps, we propose DePSAC -- a Delay and Profit-aware Slice Admission Control scheme. Our DRL-based approach incorporates a delay-aware reward function, where penalties due to service delay incentivize the prioritization of latency-critical slices such as URLLC. Additionally, we employ Boltzmann exploration to achieve smoother and faster convergence. We implement and evaluate DePSAC on a simulated 5G core network substrate with realistic Network Slice Request (NSLR) arrival patterns. Experimental results demonstrate that our method outperforms the DSARA baseline in terms of overall profit, reduced URLLC slice delays, improved acceptance rates, and improved resource consumption. These findings validate the effectiveness of the proposed DePSAC in achieving better QoS-profit trade-offs for practical 5G network slicing scenarios."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 8 figures, Accepted for publication at IEEE ANTS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08769","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-13T01:44:19Z","registered":"2025-10-13T01:44:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T01:44:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08755","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08755","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.08755","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pantea","familyName":"Karimi","name":"Karimi, Pantea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dany","familyName":"Rouhana","name":"Rouhana, Dany","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pooria","familyName":"Namyar","name":"Namyar, Pooria","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siva Kesava Reddy","familyName":"Kakarla","name":"Kakarla, Siva Kesava Reddy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Venkat","familyName":"Arun","name":"Arun, Venkat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Behnaz","familyName":"Arzani","name":"Arzani, Behnaz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Heuristic Algorithm Design with LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-09T19:13:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:07:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We posit that we can generate more robust and performant heuristics if we augment approaches using LLMs for heuristic design with tools that explain why heuristics underperform and suggestions about how to fix them. We find even simple ideas that (1) expose the LLM to instances where the heuristic underperforms; (2) explain why they occur; and (3) specialize design to regions in the input space, can produce more robust algorithms compared to existing techniques~ -- ~the heuristics we produce have a $\\sim28\\times$ better worst-case performance compared to FunSearch, improve average performance, and maintain the runtime."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08755","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-13T01:43:59Z","registered":"2025-10-13T01:44:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T01:44:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08754","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08754","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.08754","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zulfiqar","familyName":"Zaidi","name":"Zaidi, Zulfiqar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Karol","name":"Karol, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessica","familyName":"Hodgins","name":"Hodgins, Jessica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoming","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Zhaoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Whole Body Model Predictive Control for Spin-Aware Quadrupedal Table Tennis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-09T19:12:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:06:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Developing table tennis robots that mirror human speed, accuracy, and ability to predict and respond to the full range of ball spins remains a significant challenge for legged robots. To demonstrate these capabilities we present a system to play dynamic table tennis for quadrupedal robots that integrates high speed perception, trajectory prediction, and agile control. Our system uses external cameras for high-speed ball localization, physical models with learned residuals to infer spin and predict trajectories, and a novel model predictive control (MPC) formulation for agile full-body control. Notably, a continuous set of stroke strategies emerge automatically from different ball return objectives using this control paradigm. We demonstrate our system in the real world on a Spot quadruped, evaluate accuracy of each system component, and exhibit coordination through the system's ability to aim and return balls with varying spin types. As a further demonstration, the system is able to rally with human players."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to appear in IEEE ICRA 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08754","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-13T01:43:58Z","registered":"2025-10-13T01:43:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T01:43:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08664","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08664","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.08664","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianan","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Jianan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Mingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yining","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianmeng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Tianmeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xing","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huawei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Huawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Faver: Boosting LLM-based RTL Generation with Function Abstracted Verifiable Middleware"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-09T15:41:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:02:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLM-based RTL generation is an interesting research direction, as it holds the potential to liberate the least automated stage in the current chip design. However, due to the substantial semantic gap between high-level specifications and RTL, coupled with limited training data, existing models struggle with generation accuracy. Drawing on human experience, design with verification helps improving accuracy. However, as the RTL testbench data are even more scarce, it is not friendly for LLMs. Although LLMs excel at higher-level languages like Python/C, they have a huge semantic gap from RTL. When implementing the same functionality, Python/C code and hardware code differ significantly in the spatiotemporal granularity, requiring the LLM not only to consider high-level functional semantics but also to ensure the low-level details align with the circuit code. It is not an easy task. In this paper, we propose a function abstracted verifiable middleware (Faver) that streamlines RTL verification in LLM-based workflows. By mixing LLM-friendly code structures with a rule-based template, Faver decouples the details of circuit verification, allowing the LLM to focus on the functionality itself. In our experiments on the SFT model and open-source models, Faver improved the model's generation accuracy by up to 14%."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08664","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-13T01:41:46Z","registered":"2025-10-13T01:41:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T01:41:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08596","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.08596","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.08596","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"V. S. Raghu","familyName":"Parupudi","name":"Parupudi, V. S. Raghu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Confidence, Not Perplexity: A Better Metric for the Creative Era of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T22:14:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:00:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reference-free metrics like self-perplexity are strongly biased against creative text generation. We propose the Confidence Score (CS), derived from a model's output probability distribution, as a less biased alternative. Experiments on gpt-4o-mini show that while fluency-based metrics prefer novel responses in 0\\% of cases on 99 creative prompts, our CS does so 19% of the time, a statistically significant difference (95% CI for difference: [11.1%, 27.3%]). We also show that CS effectively distinguishes between easy, medium, and hard tasks, confirmed by non-overlapping confidence intervals. The Confidence Score thus mitigates the creativity bias of traditional metrics while retaining their core evaluative strengths, offering a more balanced assessment for modern LLMs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to AACL-IJCNLP 2025 (Eval4NLP)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.08596","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-13T01:40:06Z","registered":"2025-10-13T01:40:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T01:40:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":46,"totalPages":3,"page":3},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-12T16%3A00%3A0